.TH CJSON_LIST_PUSH 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_list_push \- append a child to an array node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

ccol_retval_t cjson_list_push(cjson arr, cjson child);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Appends child to the end of arr.  On success the array owns child and
the caller must not free it.

One return code carries one ownership rule:

  ccol_invalid_args always means that the call rejected its arguments
  and touched nothing.  child is exactly as it was before the call.
  The caller still owns it, or the container that it is already
  attached to still owns it.  That owner must still destroy it.

  Every other non-success code (ccol_not_enough_memory,
  ccol_container_full) always means that ownership moved to this
  call.  The call already deep-freed child.  A second free of child
  is a double free.

These are the ccol_invalid_args rejections.  child is NULL.  child is
already attached to a list parent or a dictionary parent.  child is
arr itself.  arr is NULL or is not a CJSON_LIST.  There is one more
rejection.  arr is already reachable when you descend from child.
To attach child would make arr a new ancestor of child.  arr would
also stay a descendant of child.  This is a cycle.

The library cannot always complete that last check when memory is
low.  It then returns ccol_not_enough_memory.  It does not take the
risk of an undetected cycle.  This call deep-frees child, in the
same way as when the insert itself fails for lack of memory.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
arr    Target list node (must be CJSON_LIST).
child  Node to append.  It must not already be attached somewhere
       else.  Its own subtree must not already contain arr.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success, ccol_invalid_args, ccol_not_enough_memory, or
ccol_container_full if arr has already reached its maximum element
count.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A node that cjson_get(), cjson_list_get() or cjson_dictionary_get()
returns is a borrowed reference into a tree that already exists.  It
is not a new node that the caller owns.  Never give such a node as
child.  Use cjson_clone() first to get an independent copy.  That
copy is safe to push.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_list_get (3),
.BR cjson_create_list (3),
.BR cjson_clone (3)
